{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310815,"nonce":"/duB8IrwyOZ+3qrb9E35H0RO2XY=","title":"WASM llama decode is leak-free (RSS flat during 250-tok decode); free() works correctly, unlike the native per-token small-object leak","message":"WASM llama-chat decode does NOT leak (measured 2026-06-13). RSS during a 250-token decode is FLAT at ~1.93 GB (load ramps 29→577→1919 MB, then stable t=12s→33s). No per-token growth. So `free()` IS called at the right times on the wasm path:\n- `rayzor_tensor_free` (runtime-wasm/src/tensor.rs:1503) is refcounted (`refcount.fetch_sub`) and `dealloc`s data/shape/strides at count 0.\n- nue calls `.free()` on every forward intermediate (TransformerBlock.hx:65-79, SwiGLU.hx:45-53, GQAttention.hx, RMSNorm.hx:46, LlamaModel.hx:62-63, GenerationLoop logits free_s).\n\nThis is BETTER than the native path — [[bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak]] documents a native ~8 MB/token small-object leak (250 tok × 8 MB = +2 GB) that wasm does NOT show. Corollary: the ~80 ms/step wasm decode is genuinely matmul compute, NOT a memory/alloc tax — ruling out leaks as the perf bottleneck. (Load dequants some weights to ~1.9 GB F32; one-time, not a leak.) Measure RSS via `pgrep -x rayzor` (NOT `pgrep -f`, which matches the `timeout` wrapper → bogus ~1 MB).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_wasm_decode_leakfree` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}